---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import release from '../../data/release-manifest.json';
---

<Layout
  title="Varve on ChromeOS — Varve Docs"
  description="Install Varve on a Chromebook through ChromeOS's Linux development environment (Crostini): exact package, checksum verification, file sharing, updates, and uninstall."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / ChromeOS
      </nav>

      <h1>Varve on ChromeOS</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        ChromeOS can run Varve as a Linux application through the built-in Linux
        development environment (Crostini). This is a separate route from the
        native Chrome browser and from Android apps.
      </p>

      <aside class="docs-note">
        <strong>Status: experimental.</strong> Package and dependency behavior for the
        current ARM64 and x86_64 Linux builds is verified, but no physical Chromebook run has
        been recorded yet, so rendering, pen/touch input, and suspend/resume are not proven.
        Google's Linux environment is also documented as not supporting hardware
        acceleration, including GPU and video decode. Treat this page as a tested install
        contract, not a support claim. See the{' '}
        <a href={sitePath('/download')}>download page</a> for the current release.
      </aside>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Before you start</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Enable Linux.</strong> Select the time at the bottom right, then
            <strong>Settings</strong> &rarr; <strong>About ChromeOS</strong> &rarr;
            <strong>Developers</strong> &rarr; next to "Linux development environment",
            select <strong>Set up</strong>. ChromeOS installs a sandboxed Debian environment
            and opens a Terminal; setup can take ten minutes or more.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Check the architecture.</strong> Open the Terminal and run
            <code class="code-block">uname -m</code>. ARM Chromebooks print
            <code>aarch64</code>; Intel/AMD Chromebooks print <code>x86_64</code>. Download
            the matching build — do not mix them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Check the container.</strong>
            <code class="code-block">cat /etc/os-release</code> shows the Debian version.
            Varve needs glibc 2.35 or newer, which Debian 12 (bookworm) and Debian 13
            (trixie) both satisfy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Leave room.</strong> The package is about 49 MB download and 85 MB
            installed, plus the WebKitGTK/GTK system dependencies (a few hundred MB) and any
            optional AI models you download later. Keep more than 2 GB free in Linux.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If the Linux option is missing or disabled, the device does not support it or a
          work or school administrator has turned it off. Contact the administrator; there
          is no supported bypass.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Download the right package</h2>
        <p>
          The Debian package is the recommended format on ChromeOS, because the Linux
          environment is Debian-based. Table below shows the current release
          (v{release.version}).
        </p>
        <div class="docs-table-wrap">
          <table class="docs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Your device</th>
                <th>File</th>
                <th>Size</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>ARM Chromebook (<code>aarch64</code>)</td>
                <td><code>Varve-{release.version}-linux-aarch64.deb</code></td>
                <td>49.0 MB</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Intel/AMD Chromebook (<code>x86_64</code>)</td>
                <td><code>Varve-{release.version}-linux-x86_64.deb</code></td>
                <td>49.9 MB</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          An AppImage also exists, but on ChromeOS it needs FUSE2 and the host WebKitGTK
          libraries. The published v0.2.1 AppImages are additionally missing the bundled
          native ONNX Runtime because of a packaging defect (fixed for the next release),
          so native AI features fall back there. Use the <code>.deb</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Download, verify, and install</h2>
        <p>
          Run these commands in the Linux Terminal. The checksum check proves the file
          matches the published release data; like every Linux artifact, it is not
          code-signed.
        </p>
        <code class="code-block">mkdir -p ~/Downloads/varve &amp;&amp; cd ~/Downloads/varve</code>
        <code class="code-block">curl -fLO https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/releases/download/v{release.version}/Varve-{release.version}-linux-aarch64.deb</code>
        <code class="code-block">curl -fLO https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/releases/download/v{release.version}/SHA256SUMS.txt</code>
        <code class="code-block">sha256sum -c --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS.txt</code>
        <p>
          The expected result is <code>Varve-{release.version}-linux-aarch64.deb: OK</code>.
          Then install — this is a system modification, so it asks for your password:
        </p>
        <code class="code-block">sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install ./Varve-{release.version}-linux-aarch64.deb</code>
        <p>
          Use <code>apt install ./file.deb</code>, not <code>dpkg -i</code>: only apt
          resolves the WebKitGTK and GTK dependencies. Do not use forced installs if it
          fails; report the error instead.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>First launch and where files live</h2>
        <p>
          Search for <strong>Varve</strong> in the Launcher, or run
          <code>varve-desktop</code> in the Terminal. Create a document, draw, save with
          Ctrl+S, close it, and reopen it to confirm the round trip.
        </p>
        <div class="docs-table-wrap">
          <table class="docs-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Location</th>
                <th>Visible to Linux</th>
                <th>Survives removing Linux</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Linux files</strong> (Files app)</td>
                <td>Yes</td>
                <td>No</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>My files / Downloads / Drive</td>
                <td>Only after <strong>Share with Linux</strong>, then under <code>/mnt/chromeos</code></td>
                <td>Yes</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Keep project documents in a shared My files or Drive folder, not only in the Linux
          container. Sharing is explicit and per-folder; Drive-backed files are not always
          available offline.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Updates and uninstall</h2>
        <p>
          There is no apt repository for Varve, so <code>apt upgrade</code> will not update
          it. To update, download the newer <code>.deb</code> from the release page, verify
          its checksum, and install it over the old version; documents and settings are
          preserved. Rolling back means installing an older <code>.deb</code>, which only
          works while the document format remains compatible — keep backups.
        </p>
        <p>
          To remove the application without touching your documents:
          <code class="code-block">sudo apt remove varve</code>. App settings and recovery
          records live under <code>~/.local/share/dev.varve.desktop</code> and
          <code>~/.config/dev.varve.desktop</code>; remove those only if you actually want
          them gone. Removing the Linux environment itself deletes everything held inside
          Linux, so back up first with ChromeOS's Linux backup (.tini) flow. Browser/PWA
          data is separate and unaffected.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Known ChromeOS limitations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No hardware acceleration</strong> in the Linux environment (Google
            documents GPU and video decode as unsupported), so expect software rendering;
            the Mali GPU and the NPU are not used by Varve here.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Printing</strong> depends on CUPS inside the container, which is often
            absent. Export a PDF from Varve and print from ChromeOS instead.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Fonts</strong> are the container's fontconfig set, not the host's. Add
            fonts inside Linux if a project needs them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Pen and touch</strong> are forwarded as pointer events; the USI Pen 2
            and detachable-keyboard behavior have not been verified on hardware.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Lifecycle:</strong> Linux processes stop when you log out and the
            container is suspended when idle. Save before closing the lid; suspend/resume
            with an open document is unverified.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If the window is blank, confirm a WebKit web process is alive
          (<code>pgrep -af WebKitWebProcess</code>) and capture the Terminal output when
          launching <code>varve-desktop</code>. A reversible diagnostic for renderer
          problems is <code>WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1 varve-desktop</code>; unset it
          after testing. Never disable system security features.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Get the current release</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href={sitePath('/download')}>download page</a> lists checksums and sizes for
          every platform. For problems, see the{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/support/troubleshooting')}>troubleshooting guide</a> or{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/support/report-issue')}>report an issue</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .breadcrumb a,
  .docs-section a,
  .docs-cta a,
  .docs-note a {
    color: var(--text-link);
  }

  .docs-page h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
    font-family: var(--font-editorial);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-intro {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .docs-note {
    margin: -1rem 0 3rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .docs-note strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .docs-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section p,
  .docs-section li {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .docs-section p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .docs-section ol,
  .docs-section ul {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .docs-section li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .docs-section strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section code.code-block {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .docs-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-control);
  }

  .docs-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .docs-table th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    padding: 0.6rem 0.875rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-default);
    background: var(--surface-card);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .docs-table td {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.875rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .docs-table tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .docs-table td strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-cta {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 3rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }

  .docs-cta h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-cta p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
</style>
